# WHO PAID FOR VOLUNTEER-RUN RELAY INFRASTRUCTURE — MEASURED

All figures pulled 2026-09-04. "NOT REACHED" = I could not retrieve it; nothing below is filled from memory.

---

## 1. TOR — the volunteer relay floor, funded by a foundation that never paid the relays

### 1a. The structural split (the key finding)
The Tor Project Inc. funds **software**, not **relays**. Relay operators pay their own hosting. `https://community.torproject.org/relay/` states only: *"The Tor network relies on volunteers to donate bandwidth."* No compensation, no cost-sharing, no bandwidth-purchase program is described. So there are **two separate funding stories**: the 501(c)(3) (well funded, US-government-dominated) and the relay floor (funded by nobody centrally).

### 1b. Tor Project Inc. finances — Form 990, EIN 20-8096820
Source: ProPublica Nonprofit Explorer API `https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/api/v2/organizations/208096820.json`; PDFs at `https://www.torproject.org/about/reports/`.

| Tax yr | Total revenue | Total expenses | Net assets EOY |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2011 | $1,387,054 | $1,601,224 | $833,134 |
| 2013 | $2,872,929 | $2,431,941 | $1,700,238 |
| 2015 | $3,278,452 | $2,850,671 | $2,130,326 |
| 2017 | $4,130,882 | $4,078,419 | $2,458,686 |
| 2018 (6-mo stub, Jan 1–Jun 30 2018) | $1,759,348 | $2,502,854 | $1,675,497 |
| 2019 (FY Jul19–Jun20) | $4,868,034 | $5,450,934 | $836,494 |
| 2021 (FY Jul21–Jun22) | $7,413,548 | $3,987,743 | $4,406,515 |
| 2023 (FY Jul23–Jun24) | $8,470,139 | $6,689,589 | $7,145,619 |

Note the **FY2019 stress point**: expenses exceeded revenue by $582,900 and net assets fell from $1.68M to $836K in one year.

### 1c. US-government share — Form 990 Part VIII line 1e "Government grants (contributions)", computed against line 12 total revenue
(extracted with `pdftotext -layout` from the PDFs on torproject.org/about/reports/)

| Fiscal year | Total revenue | Gov't grants (1e) | Gov't % |
|---|---|---|---|
| FY Jul18–Jun19 | $4,868,034 | $2,025,910 | **41.6%** |
| FY Jul19–Jun20 | $4,400,782 | $2,372,145 | **53.9%** |
| FY Jul20–Jun21 | $7,413,548 | $2,829,250 | **38.2%** |
| FY Jul21–Jun22 | $5,999,891 | $3,058,724 | **51.0%** |
| FY Jul22–Jun23 | $8,470,139 | $3,684,992 | **43.5%** |
| FY Jul23–Jun24 | $7,287,566 | $2,135,763 | **29.3%** |

CY2017 line 1e was only $681,212 (16.5%), but that year also booked **$1,446,032 of "FEE FOR SERVICES" program service revenue** (Part VIII line 2a, Business Code 900099) — historically US-gov subcontracting (SRI International). Program-service revenue runs $1.3M–$2.8M/yr from 2011–2016 and goes to **$0 from FY2020 onward**, i.e. the funding form changed from contract to grant. Treat pre-2018 "government %" from 1e alone as an undercount.

Directly quoted from the **2020-2021 Annual Report** (`https://www.torproject.org/static/findoc/2020-2021-TorProject-Annual-Report.pdf`, p.11):
> Total Revenue: $7,412,081 — U.S. Government 38.17% $2,829,250 · Individual Donations 36.22% $2,684,390 · Private Foundations 16.15% $1,196,763 · Non-U.S. Government 5.07% $375,809 · Corporate 2.89% $213,950 · Other 1.51% $111,919
> Total Expenses: $3,987,743 — Program Services 87.2% $3,477,521 · Fundraising 7.3% $292,902 · Administration 5.4% $217,320

**2012 Annual Report** (`.../2012-TorProject-Annual-Report.pdf`) 2011 income pie: **U.S. Government based income 60%**, three other slices 18/18/4. Revenue trajectory it prints: 2009 $1,253,241 → 2010 $1,574,119 → 2011 $1,681,101 → 2012 "over $2 million (unaudited)".

I did **NOT REACH** an explicit "~85% in 2015" statement; the blog transparency posts (`blog.torproject.org/transparency-openness-and-our-*-finances`) all return 404 in 2026 — the blog was reorganized. The 2015 figure remains unverified here.

### 1d. Relay counts and bandwidth over time — MEASURED
`https://metrics.torproject.org/networksize.csv?start=2007-01-01&end=2026-09-01`, `relayflags.csv`, `bandwidth.csv` (annual means computed from daily rows).

| Year | relays (mean) | bridges | Exit relays | Exit % of running | Advertised BW | Consumed BW |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2007 | 1,300 | — | 603 | 46.4% | 2.06 Gbit/s | — |
| 2010 | 1,752 | 478 | 723 | 41.3% | 5.10 | 3.22 |
| 2013 | 3,862 | 1,705 | 883 | 22.9% | 36.57 | 21.79 |
| 2014 | 5,713 | 3,334 | 1,041 | 18.2% | 83.46 | 40.07 |
| 2016 | 7,118 | 2,515 | 930 | 13.1% | 184.61 | 76.66 |
| **2017** | 6,983 | 2,784 | **817** | **11.7%** ← floor | 222.19 | 102.97 |
| 2018 | 6,339 | 1,336 | 832 | 13.1% | 261.48 | 122.14 |
| 2020 | 6,588 | 1,449 | 1,240 | 18.8% | 489.71 | 229.08 |
| 2022 | 6,669 | 2,551 | 1,503 | 22.5% | 678.43 | 278.67 |
| 2024 | 7,658 | 1,896 | 2,274 | 29.7% | 765.31 | 320.25 |
| 2026 (to Sep 1) | 9,853 | 2,665 | 2,930 | 29.7% | 1,320.89 | 462.30 |

Last row of the CSV: `2026-09-01,10137,2545`.

**The scissors**: relay count grew 5.4× from 2007→2026 while advertised bandwidth grew **641×** (2.06 → 1,320.89 Gbit/s). The volunteer body count is nearly flat 2014–2023 (6.3k–7.1k) while carried traffic tripled. Growth came from *bigger* relays, not *more* volunteers.

**Exit scarcity**: exits fell from 46.4% of relays (2007) to **11.7% (2017)**, the measured floor — the legally-exposed role is the one volunteers refuse. It has since recovered to ~29.7%, driven by a handful of professionalized non-profits (below), not by dispersion.

### 1e. Exit concentration — MEASURED, 2026-09-04
Onionoo API `https://onionoo.torproject.org/details?type=relay&running=true&flag=Exit&fields=...` — 3,215 running exit relays, total observed exit bandwidth **413.4 Gbit/s**; 9,451 total running relays (`onionoo.../summary`).

Exit probability by operator contact string:
- `nothingtohide.nl` — **0.2170 (21.7% of all Tor exit traffic, one operator)**
- Artikel10 (artikel10.org) — 0.0812
- tor@r0cket.net — 0.0639
- Foundation for Applied Privacy — 0.0534
- Quetzalcoatl_relays — 0.0504
- DFRI (Sweden) — 0.0475
- F3 Netze e.V. — 0.0437
- Quintex Alliance — 0.0360; sabotage.net 0.0353; relayon.org 0.0332; **Nos Oignons 0.0264**; KeFF 0.0260
- **Top 12 contacts = 71.4% of exit probability.** Top 10 ASes = 71.3% (AS215125 "Church of Cyberology" 21.7%; AS60729 "Stiftung Erneuerbare Freiheit" 15.0%).
- 50 exit relays run with **no contact info at all**.

So the "volunteer" exit floor is in practice ~12 funded non-profits. Their money comes from *other* foundations, not from Tor Project: torservers.net (`https://www.torservers.net/`) lists Wau Holland Foundation, Hivos Digital Defenders, Renewable Freedom Foundation, Access Now, RSF, IWPR as institutional supporters — **no dollar figures published (NOT REACHED)**. Wau Holland's projects page (`https://www.wauland.de/en/projects/`) lists "Anonymity networks" and "Decentralized communication networks (XMPP)" but **publishes no per-project amounts (NOT REACHED)**. Nos Oignons (`https://nos-oignons.net/`) shows a live 2026 funding campaign ("toujours besoin de soutien !") and relays "approaching 1000 Mb/s" at Tetaneutral.net; **no budget figures on the page (NOT REACHED)**.

Sponsor list `https://www.torproject.org/about/sponsors/` discloses **no amounts for any sponsor, any year** — confirmed negative.

