## 2. I2P — essentially no financial record exists

- `https://i2p.net/en/about/hall-of-fame`: states only *"I2P is built entirely by volunteers"* and solicits donations. **No donation totals, no balance, no expenses — the page that used to be a donation ledger now carries no numbers. NOT REACHED / does not exist publicly.**
- Router counts, MEASURED, from papers:
  - *An Empirical Study of the I2P Anonymity Network and its Censorship Resistance* (IMC'18, arXiv 1809.09086, 2018-09-24): **"around 32K active I2P peers in the network on a daily basis"**, of which **14K behind NAT/firewalls**. A censor running only **10 routers could block >95% of peer IP addresses**; >70% blocking causes significant latency, >90% renders the network unusable.
  - *Mapping The Invisible Internet: Framework and Dataset* (arXiv 2506.18159, v1 2025-06-22, v2 2025-11-05): dataset documents **"over 50,000 nodes"** including FastSet/high-capacity subsets, via router consoles + netDb + passive monitoring; published on Zenodo.
  - Others found: arXiv 2509.18572 (centrality-based attack resilience), 1907.07120 (*Measuring I2P Censorship at a Global Scale*, 2019-07-16).
- No I2P funding model failed, because there was never a funded model to fail. I2P is the control case: ~32k–50k routers with **zero** central budget.

---

## 3. SOVRIN FOUNDATION — the full arc, measured to the dollar

Source: the Foundation's own annual reports, `https://sovrin.org/annual-reports/` (all extracted via pdftotext).

**Statement of Support and Expense (US$ thousands):**

| Year | Support & revenue | Cost & expense | Excess/(deficit) | Cash EOY |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2018 | **2,956** | 2,569 | +387 | — |
| 2019 | **1,897** | **3,931** | **(2,034)** | — |
| 2020 | **453** | 967 | (514) | 46 |
| 2021 | **91** | 134 | (43) | 26 |
| 2022 | **120** | 106 | +14 | 41 |
| 2023 | **94** | 84 | +9 | 96 |

Accumulated earned deficit: **(US$2,056k)** at end-2021, (2,043) 2022, (2,033) 2023.

**Where the early money came from** (2019 report, Financial Report): *"receiving a total of $4.3 million ($3.2 million in 2017 & 2018 and $1.1 million in 2019) donated by Evernym"* from SAFT/PFTSA **token pre-sales**. 2019 revenue breakdown: $657,000 Sovrin Alliance donations, $165,000 training/SSI Incubator, **$2,500 network use fees**. 2019 expenses: Legal $1.5M (of which **$1.1M on regulatory advice for public token distribution**), Payroll & Contract Services $2.1M (payroll ~15 people $1.7M, ~8 contractors $0.4M), Facilities/Ops $0.3M. Year-end 2019: *"liabilities exceeded its assets by $1.5 million"*; a **$500,000 loan**, 2-year term, 5% interest, taken December 2019.

The 2019 report's own warning, verbatim:
> "Unless the Foundation is able to obtain stable funding through donations, or funds from pre-public sale of tokens, the Foundation may have to terminate substantially all paid employees and return to a volunteer-only operation."

**That is exactly what happened, on 2020-03-13.** 2020 report: *"On March 13, 2020, Sovrin Foundation's Executive Director & CEO, Heather Dahl, announced that the organization moves to volunteer staff operations."* A 12-volunteer Transition Committee coined **"Minimum Viable Foundation" (MVF)**. Treasurer Philippe Page, 2020 report:
> "In a matter of weeks, they brought the 2019 average monthly burn rate of **$328,000** down to approximately **$4,000**. A rate sustained for the rest of 2020."
> "When the new board took office, the debt was estimated to **$2.3 million**. Over two thirds was coming from unpaid bills from service providers, and the Foundation signed three forbearance agreements with its three most prominent ones." Plus a **$0.5M private loan** due Dec 2021 and a **$0.2M state Small Business Loan, 30-year duration**.

**Ledger revenue was never remotely enough**: 2020 write revenue **$17,000** (a "400% increase YoY"); 2021 YTD-September **$55,851**; full-2021 MainNet transaction revenue **$76,000 (+450%)**. Fee schedule changes (2021 report): membership fee $5,600/yr → **MainNet Endorser DID annual fee $5,000**; StagingNet Endorser DID **$500 per 6 months**. 2022: a 90% discount on ledger write fees meant *"Sovrin hereby subsidized the industry with more than $300K"* — i.e. the utility's own pricing gave away three times its annual revenue. Discount removed 1 May 2023 (`https://sovrin.org/announcement-removal-of-90-discount-on-testnet-transactions-by-1-may-2023/`).

**Steward / node counts (MEASURED):**
- Network launched **July 2017 with 10 Founding Stewards** (live 2017-07-31).
- 2018: Stewardship program formalized; *"Approved of new Stewards, scaling the number by nearly 300%"*, *"over 60 Founding Stewards in the past year."*
- 2020: **12 new Stewards added, only 6 actually spun up a node; 4 MainNet Stewards removed their nodes → net gain of 2.** End-2020: MainNet 18 nodes, StagingNet 14, BuilderNet 18 = **50 active nodes**.
- 2021: **4 added, 4 turned off** → still MainNet 18 / Staging 14 / Builder 18 = **50 nodes**. Uptime 100%.
- 2023: *"over four years of 100% uptime"*; the Board **lowered the technical requirements for Steward nodes** specifically *"to reduce the expenses associated with running cloud instances"* and to give *"Stewards the opportunity to lessen the resources (and expenses) associated with their network node."*

**The end.** `https://sovrin.org/sovrin-foundation-mainnet-ledger-shutdown-likely-on-or-before-march-31-2025/` — announced **2025-02-08**, MainNet shutdown *likely on or before 2025-03-31*. Stated causes: (1) *"In 2024, no new Transaction Endorsers joined, and efforts to revitalize interest did not increase engagement or revenue"*; (2) regulatory ambiguity deterring investment; (3) TestNet technical problems consuming the resources needed for Steward node upgrades; (4) **limited Steward involvement in governance**, compounded by the **$2 million debt inherited from the previous Board**.

`https://sovrin.org/the-sovrin-foundation-has-been-dissolved-but-sovrin-mainnet-remains/` — the Foundation *"was officially dissolved by the State of Utah on **May 21, 2025**."* **Trinsic is now the sole Steward**, running the whole MainNet on **a single virtual cloud server in read-only configuration**. Just under **$10,000** was paid to the SBA to wind down the COVID-19 EIDL loan. No trustee was compensated; dissolution work was volunteer.

**Failure shape:** ~60 institutional stewards each donating a node cost nothing to join and nothing to leave. Node operation was never the expensive part — **governance, legal, and payroll were**, and no steward paid for those. When the token exit vanished, the Foundation shed 99% of its burn rate in weeks and survived six more years as a $90k/yr shell before the stewards' collective indifference (no new endorsers in 2024, "limited Steward involvement in governance") closed it. 60 stewards → 1.

---

## 4. USENET / NNTP — the cost curve outran the ISPs

Source: `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Usenet` (+ #Binary_content).

**Daily feed volume, MEASURED:**

| Date | Volume/day |
|---|---|
| 1996 Dec | 4.5 GiB |
| 1997 Jul | 9 GiB |
| 1998 Jan | 12 GiB |
| 2000 Jan | 82 GiB |
| 2005 Jan | 2.27 TiB |
| 2010 Jan | 5.42 TiB |
| 2015 Jan | 23.87 TiB |
| 2020 Jan | 62.40 TiB |
| 2023 Aug | 220 TiB |
| 2024 Feb | **274.49 TiB** |

That is **~61,000× growth in daily feed volume 1996→2024**, on a peering model where every full-feed carrier pays for the whole thing regardless of whether its own users read any of it. Current commercial retention: *"more than 12 years... more than 60 petabytes (60000 terabytes) of storage."*

**Abandonment dates and stated reasons:**
- **AOL** — early **2005**. Reason given: *"growing popularity of weblogs, chat forums and on-line conferencing"* (not cost, not law).
- **NY AG Andrew Cuomo agreement, 2008** — Verizon, Time Warner Cable and Sprint agreed to cut Usenet access over child-pornography sources. Time Warner Cable dropped Usenet **entirely**; Sprint dropped all of `alt.*`; Verizon cut back to the Big 8.
- **Verizon** — full discontinuation **2009-09-30**; later dropped `alt.binaries.*`.
- **AT&T** — **2009-07-15**, dropped `alt.binaries.*`, then full service.
- **Microsoft** — **2010-06-01**, replaced by web forums.
- **Duke University** (Usenet's birthplace) — **May 2010**, *"low usage and rising costs."*
- **UNC** — **2011-02-04**, after 32 years.
- **Comcast** — **NOT REACHED**; the Wikipedia article does not mention Comcast, and I found no other retrievable primary source for a Comcast date.

Wikipedia's own framing of the mechanism: *"some worry that the ISPs used Cuomo's campaign as an excuse to end portions of Usenet access, as it is costly for the Internet service providers and not in high demand by customers."*

**Failure shape:** volunteer/bundled peering worked while the feed was megabytes. Once carrying the feed cost real money and carried legal risk, the entities paying (ISPs, universities) had no user demand justifying it — and a single state AG's action gave every one of them cover to exit within months. Usenet itself did not die; it moved entirely to **paid commercial providers**, which is where the 274 TiB/day now lives.

---

## 5. FREENODE 2021 — federation with a single point of governance capture

**Ownership chain** (`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freenode`):
- Peer-Directed Projects Center (PDPC), Texas 501(c)(3), 2002–~2010; supported by e.g. the Linux Fund in 2007.
- After founder Rob Levin's death (Sept 2006), Christel Dahlskjaer incorporated **PDPC Limited in the UK in 2008**. It reported **£3,060 in net assets for 2009** and was **dissolved in 2013**. (The largest FOSS IRC network on earth was backed by a charity with ~£3k of assets.)
- **2017**: Dahlskjaer incorporated **Freenode Limited and transferred ownership to Andrew Lee**. HN item 2017-04-12: "PIA and Freenode joining forces" (`https://freenode.net/news/pia-fn`).

**2021 timeline:**
- **Feb 2021** — Dahlskjaer adds Shells (Lee's company) branding to the site; resigns.
- **Apr 2021** — staff elect Tom Wesley head of staff; Lee allegedly removes the announcement post.
- **2021-05-11** — Lee appoints his own infrastructure overseer, publishes accusations against staff.
- **2021-05-19** — **at least 14 volunteer staff resign**; described as a *"hostile takeover."* Libera.Chat founded **the same day**. Resignation letter: `https://p.haavard.me/407`; FAQ: `https://gist.github.com/joepie91/df80d8d36cd9d1bde46ba018af497409` (*"The network that runs at freenode.org/net/com should now be assumed to be under control of a malicious party."* — LWN 856543, 2021-05-19).
- **2021-05-26** — freenode **seizes ~700 registered channels** from projects migrating away (Gentoo notice: `https://www.gentoo.org/news/2021/05/26/gentoo-freenode-channels-hijacked.html`; LWN `https://lwn.net/Articles/857252/`).
- **2021-06-11** — FSF and GNU move official channels to Libera.
- **2021-06-13** — freenode blocks all IRCCloud users.
- **2021-06-14** — freenode **resets the network entirely**: *"none of the data from the old network... migrated over."*

**NUMBERS — netsplit.de crawler averages (via Wayback, `web.archive.org/web/<ts>id_/https://netsplit.de/networks/freenode/`), plus live fetch today:**

| Date | freenode users | freenode channels |
|---|---|---|
| **2021-04-30** (pre-takeover) | **77,905** | **42,001** |
| **2021-06-29** (6 wks later) | **41,171** | **29,252** |
| **2022-01-28** | **9,931** | **14,148** |
| **2026-09-04** (live) | **5,191** | **7,918** |

→ **−93.3% users, −81.1% channels in five years; −47% in the first six weeks.**

**Libera.Chat, from its own posts:**
- 2021-05-25, *One week of Libera Chat* (`https://libera.chat/news/one-week-of-libera-chat`): *"we've been able to reach **16,500 simultaneous connections and 20,000 registered accounts**. We entered the global top 10 within days, and are the fastest-growing IRC network ever."* 250+ project registrations completed.
- 2021-12-20, *end of 2021 review* (`https://libera.chat/news/end-of-2021-review`): peak **51,728 concurrent users**, **~21,000 open channels**, **~64,000 NickServ accounts**, **~18,000 registered channels**, **900+ registered projects**, registration backlog down from hundreds to <30.
- **2026-09-04 live (netsplit.de):** **31,519 users / 22,488 channels** — i.e. Libera today is **6.1× freenode's user count**, but is itself down ~39% from its 2021 peak.

**Funding of the successor:** Libera has no corporate owner. Its money is merch (FreeWear partnership, 2023-02-10) and Liberapay donations; the 2025-10-01 post is literally titled *"The price of good advice"* — **fundraising to pay for legal advice** (`https://libera.chat/news/advice`). 2025-04-30 post *"OSUOSL is worth saving"* — its own hosting provider needed rescuing. **Exact Libera revenue figures: NOT REACHED** (no financial report published).

**Failure shape:** freenode's server capacity was donated and federated; its *namespace and NickServ database* were not. One company (Freenode Ltd → Andrew Lee) held the single governance point, and capturing it destroyed 93% of the network. The replacement cost of the *infrastructure* was near zero — Libera rebuilt in one week — but the migration cost 20 years of channel registrations, and the successor's chronic exposure is legal fees, not servers.

---

## 6. XMPP — the federation that lost, with almost no measurement left behind

Source: `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XMPP`.
- Google Talk launched **Aug 2005**; **server-to-server federation enabled 2006-01-17**.
- **May 2013**: Google announces *"XMPP compatibility would be dropped from Google Talk for server-to-server federation, although it would retain client-to-server support"* (Hangouts). HN 2013-04-09 records a brief reopening (`http://mail.jabber.org/pipermail/operators/2013-April/001672.html`), and HN 2014-10-31 *"Google Hangouts breaks XMPP federation."*
- **Facebook** opened Chat via XMPP **Feb 2010**; **support dropped April 2014**.
- Only user metric Wikipedia carries: *"by 2003... over ten million people worldwide"* (flagged as needing update).

**Measured server-count decline over time: NOT REACHED.** I probed `xmpp.net` (200), `list.jabber.at` (200, ~150+ servers listed, **no totals, no history, no uptime series**), `compliance.conversations.im` (200), `data.xmpp.net` (**403**). Crossref and arXiv returned nothing measuring XMPP federation size; OpenAlex hit its daily budget cap mid-session (`{"error":"Rate limit exceeded"... "Resets at midnight UTC"}`) and could not be re-queried. **Conclusion as a negative finding: there is no publicly retrievable longitudinal dataset of XMPP server counts.** The federation that lost also lost its own instrumentation — which is itself the finding: nobody was funded to keep measuring it.

The one funding datapoint: Wau Holland Foundation lists *"Decentralized communication networks — Funds XMPP messaging platform operations"* as a standing project (`https://www.wauland.de/en/projects/`), amounts undisclosed.

