## 7. MASTODON — instance operating costs, published

### 7a. Mastodon gGmbH / Mastodon Inc., from the annual report PDFs
`https://joinmastodon.org/reports/Mastodon%20Annual%20Report%20{2022,2023,2024}.pdf`

| | 2021 | 2022 | 2023 | 2024 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Donations/revenue | €55.6k | **€325.9k** (+488%) | **€545k** (+65%) | **€2,191k** (gGmbH €2,111k + Inc. — reported as €2.2M total) |
| Patreon patrons | 1,428 | 7,962 → 9,603 | 7,400 (−22.9%) | — |
| Avg annual donation | €90 | €34 (−62%) | €60 (+70%) | — |
| Total costs | €23.2k (2021) | €127.1k (+448%) | €473k | **€774k** |
| — Technical hosting | — | — | **€73k** | **€84k** |
| — Personnel | €18.4k | €79.7k | €383k | **€585k** |
| — Other | €4.8k | €6.4k+ | €15k | €106k |
| Net | — | — | **+€72k** | **+€1,417k** |
| Cash | — | — | €152k | **€1,590k** |

**Hosting is the cheapest line item by an order of magnitude.** €84k/yr of technical hosting runs **mastodon.social + mastodon.online**; €585k goes to six people. Payroll ran ~7× infrastructure in 2024.

2024 revenue sources, verbatim: *"Patreon (Germany): **€248,531** from recurring small [donations]"*; *"Direct Donations (US): **$1,582,095** including major gifts"* — one donor gave **$1,500,000 specifically to support restructuring**, one **$100,000** for the web UI, Craig Newmark **$50,000**; grants from NLnet/NGI **€30k**; commercial contracts including **hosting the European Commission's Mastodon instance**; merchandise **€36,152 from 718 orders**. 2023's largest single gift: **$100,000 from Sujitech**.

So: **86% of Mastodon's 2024 revenue was two philanthropic gifts.** The recurring, community-funded base (Patreon) was €248.5k — enough to cover hosting (€84k) but only 42% of payroll.

### 7b. Fleet-wide scale (from the 2024 report, via Mastodon's own crawler of `/api/v1/instance` stats)
- **2024-01-01: 9,446 servers, 8.67M registered accounts, 1.51M monthly active users.**
- **2024-12-31: 8,851 servers (−6.3%), 9.10M registered accounts, 938,000 MAU (−38%).**
- mastodon.social + mastodon.online gained **537,844 new users** in 2024; **113,728 local accounts suspended**.

The server count is *shrinking* while account count grows — i.e. consolidation onto the funded flagship instances, and MAU fell 38% in one year.

### 7c. Community funding, MEASURED via OpenCollective API (`https://api.opencollective.com/graphql/v2`)
- **Mastodon**: totalAmountReceived **$135,038.13**, yearlyBudget **$7,550.04**, balance $24,091.96. (Lifetime OpenCollective ≈ 6% of one year's revenue.) Largest org donor Samsung NEXT $70,000 since 2019.
- **Hachyderm**: totalAmountReceived **$785**, yearlyBudget **$701**, balance $713.76. For an instance that took **30,000 users in 30 days** (Nov 2022) — that is **$0.026 per user, lifetime**.

### 7d. A single instance's real infrastructure — Hachyderm
`https://community.hachyderm.io/blog/2022/12/03/leaving-the-basement/`: 30,000 users in 30 days (~1 new user every 1.5 min) on four donated Dell PowerEdge boxes in a residential basement (R630, 48 cores/128 GB; 3× R620, 32 cores/64 GB each), 200 Mbps advertised egress (217 Mbps achieved), **999.80 GiB RX/TX on the peak day 2022-11-21**, ~1.2 TB storage migrated to DigitalOcean. **The post publishes no dollar figures** — the cost was absorbed personally and by a vendor credit (`https://www.digitalocean.com/blog/digitalocean-spaces-mastodon-hachyderm`). Grew to **38,000+ users** by 2023 (`https://thenewstack.io/how-hashyderm-scaled-up-after-elon-musk-twitter-takeover/`); the founder stepped down 2023-04-30; died Aug 2023.

**Failure shape (Mastodon):** hosting is genuinely cheap and volunteers really can absorb it — that part works. What is not funded is *people*: moderation, legal, and the single admin. The measured consequence is consolidation (9,446 → 8,851 servers in 12 months) toward the two instances that have a €84k hosting budget and six salaried staff behind them, funded by two one-off gifts.

---

## Cross-case measured summary

| System | Who actually paid | Peak scale | What broke | Date |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Tor Project Inc. | US government 29–54% of revenue every measured year FY2019–FY2024 | $8.47M revenue FY2022-23 | nothing yet — but the org has never paid a relay | — |
| Tor relay floor | operators + ~12 foundation-backed non-profits | 10,137 relays, 1,321 Gbit/s (2026) | exits fell to 11.7% of relays (2017); **1 operator = 21.7% of exit traffic today** | ongoing |
| I2P | nobody | ~32k daily peers (2018), 50k nodes catalogued (2025) | no budget ever existed to fail; 10 routers can block 95% of peer IPs | — |
| Sovrin | Evernym token pre-sales $4.3M | ~60 stewards, 50 nodes | 2019 deficit $2.03M; burn $328k/mo → $4k/mo in weeks; dissolved | 2020-03-13 → 2025-05-21 |
| Usenet | ISPs, bundled | 274.49 TiB/day (2024-02) | feed cost + AG action; every major ISP exited 2005–2010 | 2005–2011 |
| freenode | a UK charity with **£3,060 net assets**, then one owner | 77,905 users (2021-04-30) | governance capture → 93% user loss | 2021-05-19 |
| XMPP | Google/Facebook federation, then withdrawal | 10M users (2003) | s2s federation dropped May 2013 / Apr 2014 | 2013–2015 |
| Mastodon | 2 gifts = 86% of 2024 revenue | 9,446 servers, 1.51M MAU (2024-01) | −6.3% servers, −38% MAU in 2024 | ongoing |

**NOT REACHED, explicitly:** Tor's blog transparency posts (all 404 in 2026) and therefore the widely-cited "85% US government in 2015"; torservers.net / Wau Holland / Nos Oignons dollar figures; any Comcast Usenet-drop date; Libera.Chat revenue; any longitudinal XMPP server-count dataset; I2P financials of any kind. OpenAlex exhausted its daily budget mid-session and WebSearch was off-limits throughout.
